    {
      "id": "BSM-02",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-binary-similitude-jacobian",
      "title": "Each fibre acquires the quotient of the two pushforward factors",
      "statement": "Let aᵥ and bᵥ scale ambient Haar measure under γ and scalar Haar measure under multiplication by λ=β′/β. The local fibre transforms by aᵥ/bᵥ. The infinite-place quotient times the product of all finite-place quotients equals one. Consequently the represented length β′ has an Euler factorization with the same κ as β.",
      "hypotheses": "For a fixed CM extension E/F, the binary application uses a nondegenerate anisotropic Hermitian form, standard additive Haar measures in the chosen base-field coordinates, a Haar measure and fundamental domain for the adelic unitary quotient, and the normalized local Gram-fibre families. Assume the two rational spheres are nonempty and EP(β,κ) holds for κ≥0. Include all exceptional finite places in the restricted test.",
      "definitions": [
        "ambient pushforward",
        "length pushforward",
        "Gram-fibre measure",
        "restricted product test"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "lifts-binary-fibre-jacobian"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "BSM-01"
      ],
      "correspondence": "The Gram map commutes with γ on vectors and λ on lengths. Transport and uniqueness of the fibre family identify the quotient of moduli. Rational linear coordinates give the ambient product formula. Two scalar changes compose to congruence by the one-by-one matrix [λ], giving the target product formula by positive square roots. Almost-everywhere preservation of the standard lattice transports the Euler tests. For ℚ(i), J=I₂ and γ with rows (1,i),(i,1), the two nontrivial triples (ambient,target,fibre) are (1/4,1/2,1/2) at infinity and (4,2,2) at 2.",
