    {
      "id": "BSM-03",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-binary-similitude-empty",
      "title": "Null factors and missing lengths complete the hypothesis of the counting theorem",
      "statement": "An initially vanishing local measure gives the normalized zero factorization at once. Otherwise a represented anchor exists, and the same κ works for every nonzero scalar length, including unrepresented ones. The separate counting theorem now identifies κ with one.",
      "hypotheses": "For a fixed CM extension E/F, the binary application uses a nondegenerate anisotropic Hermitian form, standard additive Haar measures in the chosen base-field coordinates, a Haar measure and fundamental domain for the adelic unitary quotient, and the normalized local Gram-fibre families. For each individual fibre the prior mass theorem supplies finiteness of its standard finite lattice masses, summability of their absolute deviations from one, and a factorization with some nonnegative constant. The representation principle is for a binary Hermitian form and a nonzero length.",
      "definitions": [
        "represented length",
        "empty rational fibre",
        "archimedean signature obstruction"
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "dependencies": [
        "BSM-02",
        "BEQ-04"
      ],
      "correspondence": "On the original length, a zero local measure implies zero theta measure; any prescribed coefficient then works by the zero-measure Euler lemma. Otherwise nonzero infinite fibre gives a rational point. For each other unrepresented length, the rational sum is empty and the infinite measure vanishes. The original factorization at that length supplies the two regularity hypotheses needed to use the common coefficient. The sign obstruction follows by comparing J to diag(t,t det J), with determinant quotient t² a norm, and applying the signature classification. Singular length zero is outside the one-vector statement.",
